Reference brief:
{
  "contract": "x and y are integers or 1-D lists of integers. Support scalar/scalar, scalar/list, list/scalar and lists of the same length (including empty). Scalar/scalar returns int, all other inputs return a list. No other broadcasting is required. Values and results fit signed int64.",
  "documentation": "lcm(x1, x2, /, out=None, *, where=True, casting='same_kind', order='K', dtype=None, subok=True[, signature])\n\nReturns the lowest common multiple of ``|x1|`` and ``|x2|``\n\nParameters\n----------\nx1, x2 : array_like, int\n    Arrays of values.\n    If ``x1.shape != x2.shape``, they must be broadcastable to a common\n    shape (which becomes the shape of the output).\n\nReturns\n-------\ny : ndarray or scalar\n    The lowest common multiple of the absolute value of the inputs\n    This is a scalar if both `x1` and `x2` are scalars.\n\nSee Also\n--------\ngcd : The greatest common divisor\n\nExamples\n--------\n>>> import numpy as np\n>>> np.lcm(12, 20)\n60\n>>> np.lcm.reduce([3, 12, 20])\n60\n>>> np.lcm.reduce([40, 12, 20])\n120\n>>> np.lcm(np.arange(6), 20)\narray([ 0, 20, 20, 60, 20, 20])",
  "documentation_truncated": false,
  "group": "integer_arithmetic",
  "id": "numpy.lcm",
  "library": "numpy",
  "name": "lcm",
  "signature": "solve(x, y)",
  "version": "2.5.4"
}
